former pope benedict has asked  forgiveness for his handling of clergy  sex abuse cases in the catholic church  however benedict denied any personal or  specific wrongdoing  an independent report released in  january said that benedict had failed to  act against clerics in four cases of  alleged abuse during his time as  munich's archbishop between 1977 and  1982.  his request for forgiveness was made in  a letter published by the vatican and  read out by the former pope's  spokesperson  i can only express to all the victims of  sexual abuse my profound shame  profoundly  i've had great responsibilities in the  catholic church  and the errors that occurred in those  different places during the time of my  mandate  let's go to martin gacdy w's religious  affairs analyst in berlin for more on  this story so we've just heard that  apology from the pope but he's not  accepting responsibility for decades of  alleged abuse and misconduct as outlined  in that independent report  will that be enough for victims families  well i think it's very safe to say that  no that it will not be enough and you  know i mean if you look across the web  you already are seeing the the reactions  to what is considered to be just a usual  the usual approach of the church to new  revelations  you know i mean i think that victims  victims groups had  been essentially complaining about  repeated apologies with very little  progress in  establishing establishing new policies  and basically reforms that can protect  redress crimes committed and protect  potential victims  and this really doesn't go anywhere near  you know fulfilling those those needs  and those demands  now benedict has also commented on  accusations that he lied about  participating in a meeting about  transferring a priest alleged to have  abused children here in germany what can  you tell us about that  well the report mentions at least four  cases uh of what you know in the press  conference was referred to as misconduct  by the by the former pope or the  meredith pope so this is not something  that obviously we can we can say much  about because it's something that is  contained in a legal report and it's  essentially in the hands of  a prosecutor's office so i mean this is  something that you know the justice  justice german justice that we'll have  too we'll have to decide what is quite  clear is that um  you know wittingly unwittingly the pope  did uh benedict did lie or  at least mischaracterize  how much he knew about the case and the  cases in in in the report particularly  for cases uh and then once that the  report was released and there was a  rebuke of you know the pope saying that  he knew nothing about this case or that  he had actually  not been part of one meeting or another  then he was actually forced to retract  those those comments so the fact is that  there is now solid evidence that he  failed to reveal something that he  actually didn't know about these cases  and with that in mind if the catholic  church is looking to win back public  trust what steps need to be taken to  fully address abuses  there's been you know an increasing  demand from  victims groups and i think that legal  scholars and people different groups  among them legal scholars and academics  that had been sort of working through  questions of church reform had been  pointing to which is making the  reporting of suspicions or the reporting  of crimes mandatory  to national authorities the church of  course has resisted this and there are  still several bails of secrecy over the  protocol particularly at confessional  level so the confession is protected by  secrecy  and these are things that i think at  this point becomes quite clear that  the church not being able to police  itself  would mean that national authorities  would have to take  you know this kind of steps more  seriously and into serious consideration  martin gack dw religious affairs analyst  in berlin thanks so much for that  analysis  you
 

